% === provenance: jadud2005thesis ===
% CLAIM: The Error Quotient scores consecutive compilation-event pairs
%   (both-error +2, same error type +3, same error location +-1 line +3, same
%   edit location +1), divides each pair score by 9, and averages over pairs.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search 'error quotient novice compilation behaviour' -n
%   learnlog-metrics-eq -p dblp -p wos -p ieee -p openalex (2026-08-31);
%   thesis PDF then retrieved from the author's own site, which no provider
%   indexes: WebFetch https://jadud.com/dl/pdf/jadud-dissertation.pdf
% PICKED: primary source AND the only one carrying both the flowchart and the
%   reference implementation (App. C). The ICER'06 paper states the same
%   algorithm but without the code; every secondary restatement I checked
%   (Villamor 2020, Svabensky 2024) drops the fourth term.
% QUOTE (Sec. 4.3, p. 115): "Collate Create consecutive pairs from the events
%   in the session, eg. (e1, e2), (e2, e3), (e3, e4), up to (en-1, en).
%   Calculate Score each pair according to the algorithm presented in Figure
%   4.4. Normalize Divide the score assigned to each pair by 9 (the maximum
%   value possible for each pair). Average Sum the scores and divide by the
%   number of pairs. This average is taken as the error quotient (EQ) for the
%   session."
% QUOTE (Sec. 4.3, p. 116, footnote 1): "Compilation pairs with a STRING-EDIT
%   distance of zero are filtered out before any calculation is carried out."
% QUOTE (Sec. 4.3.1, p. 120): "we require that a 'session' have at least seven
%   compilation events, during each of which the student made some kind of
%   change to their code."
% VERIFIED: full text read (pdftotext) AND Figure 4.4 rendered to PNG at 140dpi
%   and read visually (thesis p. 116 = PDF p. 134), because the text layer
%   scrambles the flowchart. The rendered figure shows a fall-through cascade
%   with FOUR additive terms; max = 2+3+3+1 = 9, which is what makes the
%   documented divisor of 9 correct. The caption's "maximum score of 8 per
%   pair" describes the worked example, not the algorithm.
% COUNTER: scholar search 'TS=("error quotient" AND (limitation OR criticism OR
%   replication OR context))' -p wos -- surfaced Ahadi et al. 2018 (caveats,
%   free parameters) and Petersen et al. 2015 (context sensitivity); neither
%   disputes the algorithm as stated, both qualify its transferability. Claim
%   about the algorithm stands; claims about its validity are scoped below.
% DATE: 2026-08-31
@phdthesis{jadud2005thesis,
  author      = {Jadud, Matthew C.},
  title       = {An Exploration of Novice Compilation Behaviour in {BlueJ}},
  school      = {University of Kent},
  year        = {2005},
  url         = {https://jadud.com/dl/pdf/jadud-dissertation.pdf},
}

